1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,784
Every booking starts as a message. On the web console, a guest
writes the way people actually write.

2
00:00:06,864 --> 00:00:14,704
Alex describes six guests, four divers and two non-divers, arriving
November fifteenth — and stops there.

3
00:00:14,784 --> 00:00:19,864
No form. No fields to fill in. Just what he happened to say.

4
00:00:19,944 --> 00:00:21,736
Watch what comes back.

5
00:00:21,816 --> 00:00:29,992
Every fact Alex stated is already recorded — his name, the date, all
six guests, and which four of them dive.

6
00:00:30,072 --> 00:00:38,944
So the assistant asks only for what is genuinely missing: how many
nights, how many rooms, and which dates the divers dive.

7
00:00:39,024 --> 00:00:42,688
It never asks him to repeat something he already said.

8
00:00:42,768 --> 00:00:51,928
Alex fills in the gaps in one sentence — three nights, three rooms,
full board, diving on the sixteenth and seventeenth.

9
00:00:52,008 --> 00:00:55,240
All six booking details are now confirmed.

10
00:00:55,320 --> 00:01:00,520
The assistant reads them back so Alex can check every figure
himself.

11
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:06,472
And notice what it does not do: it quotes no price, and it confirms
nothing as booked.

12
00:01:06,552 --> 00:01:12,184
It says plainly that someone from the team will follow up to confirm
availability and pricing.

13
00:01:12,264 --> 00:01:19,600
That is the whole idea. The assistant handles the conversation; your
team still makes every call about money.
